.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-ML-KEM ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM-512 ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM-768 ,
.Nm EVP_PKEY-ML-KEM-1024 ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-ML-KEM-512 ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-ML-KEM-768 ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-ML-KEM-1024
.Nd ML-KEM keytype and algorithm support
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy ML-KEM-512 ,
.Sy ML-KEM-768 ,
and
.Sy ML-KEM-1024
keytypes are implemented in OpenSSL's default and FIPS providers.
.Ss Keygen Parameters
No mandatory parameters are required for generating a key pair.
To set explicit parameters, use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_params 3
after calling
.Xr EVP_PKEY_keygen_init 3 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqseed" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_SEED Ns ) <octet string>
Internally, ML-KEM generates keys using a 64-byte random value (seed),
which is the concatenation of the 32-byte
.Em d
and
.Em z
parameters described in FIPS 203. This optional parameter can be used to
set a pre-determined seed prior to keypair generation.
.Pp
Generated keys default to retaining the seed used.
The seed is also by default retained when keys are loaded from
.Sy PKCS#8
files in the seed format.
When available, the seed parameter is also used during key export and
import, with keys (by default) regenerated from the seed even when also
provided on import.
See
.Sx Provider configuration parameters
below for related controls.
.Pp
When the seed is retained, it is also available as a
.Sy gettable
parameter, and private key output to
.Sy PKCS#8
files will by default include the seed.
When the seed was not initially known, or was not retained,
.Sy PKCS#8
private key files will contain only the private key in FIPS 203
.Qo Li dk Qc
format.
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Sets properties to be used when fetching algorithm implementations used
for ML-KEM hashing operations.
.Pp
Use
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_params 3
after calling
.Xr EVP_PKEY_keygen_init 3 .
.El
.Ss Common parameters
In addition to the common parameters that all keytypes should support
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ),
.Sy ML-KEM
keys keys support the parameters listed below.
These are gettable using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_octet_string_param 3
or
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_params 3 .
They can be initialised via
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3 ,
and are returned by
.Xr EVP_PKEY_todata 3
given a suitable
.Em selection .
Once a public or private key is configured, it can no longer be
modified, nor can another key component be added.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqpub" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_PUB_KEY Ns ) <octet string>
The public key value.
.Pp
This parameter is used when importing or exporting the public key value
with the
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_todata 3
functions.
The key length and content is that of the FIPS 203 (Algorithm 16:
.Sy ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal )
.Sy ek
public key for the given ML-KEM variant.
Initial import aside, this parameter is otherwise only gettable.
.It \(dqpriv" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_PRIV_KEY Ns ) <octet string>
The private key value.
.Pp
This parameter is used when importing or exporting the private key value
with the
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_todata 3
functions.
The key length and content is that of the FIPS 203 (Algorithm 16:
.Sy ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal )
.Sy dk
private key for the given ML-KEM variant.
Initial import aside, this parameter is otherwise only gettable.
.It \(dqencoded-pub-key" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ENCODED_PUBLIC_KEY Ns ) <octet string>
Used for getting and setting the encoding of a public key.
The key format is that of
.Sy ek
in FIPS 203, Algorithm 16:
.Sy ML-KEM.KeyGen_internal .
Updates of the public and private key components are only allowed on
keys that are empty.
Once a public or private key component is set, no further changes are
allowed.
This parameter is gettable and settable (once only).
.El
.Ss Provider configuration parameters
See the description of the
.Sy -provparam
option in
.Xr openssl 1
to learn how to set provider configuration parameters in the command
line tools.
See
.Xr OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter 3
to learn how to set provider configuration options programmatically.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Qo Li ml-kem.import_pct_type Qc No ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_IMPORT_PCT_TYPE Ns ) <UTF8 string>
When an
.Sy ML-KEM
key is imported as an explict FIPS 203
.Sy dk
decapsulation key, rather than a seed, a pairwise consistency test (PCT)
is optionally performed.
By default, or when this parameter is set explicitly to
.Qo Li random Qc ,
the PCT is performed with a random entropy value for the encapsulation
step.
Setting the parameter to
.Qo Li fixed Qc ,
still runs the test, but the encapsulation entropy is a fixed 32 byte
value.
Specifying any other value of the parameter, e.g.
.Qo Li none Qc ,
skips the test.
.It Qo Li ml-kem.retain_seed Qc No ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_RETAIN_SEED Ns ) <UTF8 string>
When set to a string representing a false boolean value (see
.Xr OSSL_PROVIDER_conf_get_bool 3 Ns ),
the seed will not be retained after key generation or key import from a
seed value.
If the resulting key is then written to a PKCS#8 object, it will contain
only the FIPS 203
.Qo Li dk Qc
key.
.It Qo Li ml-kem.prefer_seed Qc No ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_PREFER_SEED Ns ) <UTF8 string>
When decoding PKCS#8 objects that contain both a seed and the FIPS 203
.Qo Li dk Qc
private key, the seed is by default used to regenerate the key, and the
companion key is ignored.
When this configuration parameter is set to a string representing a
false boolean value (see
.Xr OSSL_PROVIDER_conf_get_bool 3 Ns ),
the seed is ignored (neither used to regenerate the key, nor retained),
and the companion key is used instead.
.It Qo Li ml-kem.input_formats Qc No ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_INPUT_FORMATS Ns ) <UTF8 string>
List of enabled private key input formats when parsing PKCS#8 objects.
List elements are separated by commas and/or spaces or tabs.
The list of enabled formats can be specified in the configuration file,
as seen in the
.Sx EXAMPLES
section below, or the via the
.Sy -provparam
command-line option (see also
.Xr OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter 3 Ns ).
.Pp
Values specified on the command-line override any configuration file
settings.
By default all the supported formats are enabled.
The supported formats are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Qo Li seed-priv Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which both the FIPS 203 64-byte
.Sy (d, z)
seed and the decapsulation key
.Sy dk
are present in the private key as part of the DER encoding of the ASN.1
sequence:
.Bd -literal
    ML-KEM-PrivateKey ::= CHOICE {
      seed [0] IMPLICIT OCTET STRING (SIZE (64)),
      expandedKey OCTET STRING (SIZE (1632 | 2400 | 3168)),
      both SEQUENCE {
        seed OCTET STRING (SIZE (64)),
        expandedKey OCTET STRING (SIZE (1632 | 2400 | 3168)) } }
.Ed
.Pp
If the
.Qo Li seed-priv Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.It Qo Li seed-only Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which only the 64-byte
.Sy (d, z)
seed is present in the above sequence.
If the
.Qo Li seed-only Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.It Qo Li priv-only Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which only the FIPS 203 decapsulation key
.Sy dk
is present in the above sequence.
If the
.Qo Li priv-only Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.It Qo Li oqskeypair Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which the private key is a DER encoding of an octet string
containing the concatenaton of the FIPS 203 decapsulation key
.Sy dk
and the encapsulation key
.Sy ek .
This encoding is used in some builds of the
.Qo Li oqsprovider Qc .
If the
.Qo Li oqskeypair Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.It Qo Li bare-seed Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which the private key contains the 64-byte FIPS 204 seed
.Sy (d, z)
without any ASN.1 encapsulation.
If the
.Qo Li bare-seed Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.It Qo Li bare-priv Qc :
This format represents
.Sy PKCS#8
objects in which the private key contains the FIPS 204 decapsulation key
.Sy dk
without any ASN.1 encapsulation.
If the
.Qo Li bare-priv Qc
format is not included in the list, this format will not be recognised
on input.
.El
.It Qo Li ml-kem.output_formats Qc No ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_ML_KEM_OUTPUT_FORMATS Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Ordered list of enabled private key output formats when writing
.Sy PKCS#8
files.
List elements are separated by commas, spaces or tabs.
The list of enabled formats can be specified in the configuration file,
as seen in the
.Sx EXAMPLES
section below, or the via the
.Sy -provparam
command-line option.
.Pp
This supports the same set of formats as described under
.Qo Li ml-kem.input_formats Qc
above.
The order in which elements are listed is important, the selected format
will be the first one that is possible to output.
If the key seed is known, the first listed format will be selected.
If the key seed is not known, the first format that omits the seed will
be selected.
The default order is equivalent to
.Qo Li seed-priv Qc
first and
.Qo Li priv-only Qc
second, with both seed and key output when the seed is available, and
just the key otherwise.
If
.Qo Li seed-only Qc
is listed first, then the seed will be output without the key when
available, otherwise the output will have just the key.
If
.Qo Li priv-only Qc
is listed first, then just the key is output regardless of whether the
seed is present.
The legacy
.Qo Li oqskeypair Qc ,
.Qo Li bare-seed Qc
and
.Qo Li bare-priv Qc
formats can also be output, by listing those first.
.El
.Sh CONFORMING TO
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It FIPS 203
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
An
.Sy EVP_PKEY
context can be obtained by calling:
.Bd -literal
    EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx =
        EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name(NULL, "ML-KEM-768", NULL);
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Sy ML-KEM-768
key can be generated like this:
.Bd -literal
    pkey = EVP_PKEY_Q_keygen(NULL, NULL, "ML-KEM-768");
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Sy ML-KEM
private key in seed format can be converted to a key in the FIPS 203
.Sy dk
format by running:
.Bd -literal
    $ openssl pkey -provparam ml-kem.retain_seed=no \e
        -in seed-only.pem -out priv-only.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To generate an, e.g.,
.Sy ML-KEM-768
key, in FIPS 203
.Sy dk
format, you can run:
.Bd -literal
    $ openssl genpkey -provparam ml-kem.retain_seed=no \e
        -algorithm ml-kem-768 -out priv-only.pem
.Ed
.Pp
If you have a
.Sy PKCS#8
file with both a seed and a key, and prefer to import the companion key
rather than the seed, you can run:
.Bd -literal
    $ openssl pkey -provparam ml-kem.prefer_seed=no \e
        -in seed-priv.pem -out priv-only.pem
.Ed
.Pp
In the
.Sy openssl.cnf
file, this looks like:
.Bd -literal
    openssl_conf = openssl_init

    [openssl_init]
    providers = providers_sect

    # Can be referenced in one or more provider sections
    [ml_kem_sect]
    prefer_seed = yes
    retain_seed = yes
    # OQS legacy formats disabled
    input_formats = seed-priv, seed-only, priv-only
    # Output either the seed alone, or else the key alone
    output_formats = seed-only, priv-only

    [providers_sect]
    default = default_sect
    # Or perhaps just: base = default_sect
    base = base_sect

    [default_sect]
    ml-kem = ml_kem_sect

    [base_sect]
    ml-kem = ml_kem_sect
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr openssl-pkey 1 ,
.Xr openssl-genpkey 1 ,
.Xr EVP_KEYMGMT 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_get_raw_private_key 3 ,
